// Domain 2: Incident Response (14%)
// Schema: id, d (domain), q ("scenario\n\nquestion"), o (options), a (indices of correct options), r (one rationale per option).
// Options are shuffled at runtime, so rationales never refer to option letters.
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id: "d2-01", d: 2,
  q: "A company wants a repeatable response when Amazon GuardDuty reports that an Amazon EC2 instance in its production account is compromised. Within 5 minutes of the finding, the response must move the instance to an isolation security group, snapshot its Amazon EBS volumes, and tag it for investigation. No one may sign in to the instance, and the instance must keep running to preserve memory evidence.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an AWS Systems Manager Automation runbook with these steps. Start the runbook from an Amazon EventBridge rule that matches the GuardDuty finding type.",
      "Create an AWS Systems Manager Automation runbook with these steps and a last step that stops the instance. Start it from an EventBridge rule that matches the finding type.",
      "Create an AWS Systems Manager Automation runbook with these steps. Run the runbook from a State Manager association every 30 minutes against all of the instances.",
      "Create an AWS Config rule for the instance's security groups, with an automatic remediation that runs the AWS-DisablePublicAccessForSecurityGroup runbook."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An Automation runbook changes the security group, creates snapshots, and adds tags through AWS APIs, without access to the instance. An EventBridge rule on the finding type starts it within seconds, so every incident gets the same response.",
      "Stopping the instance clears its memory, which destroys the volatile evidence that the company wants to keep.",
      "A schedule every 30 minutes misses the 5-minute window, and it runs against every instance instead of the compromised one.",
      "This rule reacts to security group settings, not to the GuardDuty finding, and the runbook neither snapshots nor tags the instance."] },

{ id: "d2-02", d: 2,
  q: "A company's security operations team must track each AWS Security Hub CSPM finding that needs action as a work item, with an owner, a status, the affected resources, and a record of the runbooks that were run to resolve it. The team already uses AWS Systems Manager for operations work and wants to start remediation runbooks from the same place where it tracks the work.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Turn on the Security Hub CSPM integration with Systems Manager OpsCenter. Work each finding as an OpsItem, and run Automation runbooks from the OpsItem.",
      "Turn on the Security Hub CSPM integration with Systems Manager Explorer only. Review the finding counts in the Explorer widgets, and assign owners there.",
      "Create a Security Hub CSPM automation rule that sets the workflow status to NOTIFIED for findings that need action. Record the owner and runbooks in finding notes.",
      "Create an Amazon EventBridge rule that writes each finding that needs action to an Amazon DynamoDB table. Track the owner, status, and runbooks as item attributes."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["OpsCenter manages operational work items called OpsItems. Security Hub CSPM findings can become OpsItems, and each OpsItem tracks an owner, a status, related resources, and the Automation runbooks that were run from it.",
      "Explorer shows summary widgets of operations data. It does not create work items with owners and runbook history.",
      "Workflow status and free-text notes do not give each finding an owner field or a record of runbook runs, and runbooks cannot be started from a finding.",
      "A DynamoDB table is a custom tracking system that the team must build, and runbooks would still be started somewhere else."] },

{ id: "d2-03", d: 2,
  q: "A company has 80 accounts in AWS Organizations and uses AWS IAM Identity Center with an external identity provider. During an incident, responders must be able to investigate any account within minutes. The company wants no long-term credentials, no standing access for responders in normal operation, and a record of who held access during each incident.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an incident response permission set, and assign it to a responders group for all 80 accounts. Add responders to the group in the identity provider only during incidents.",
      "Create an incident response permission set, and assign it permanently to each responder user for all 80 accounts. Review AWS CloudTrail after each incident ends.",
      "Create an IAM role in each account that trusts the management account. Give each responder an IAM user in the management account that can assume the roles.",
      "Create an incident response permission set after an incident starts. Assign it to the on-call responders for the affected account, and delete it afterward."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The permission set and group assignments exist before any incident, so access works within minutes of adding a responder to the group. Group membership changes in the identity provider are recorded, and the access is removed by taking the responder out of the group.",
      "Permanent assignments give responders standing access in normal operation, which the company does not want.",
      "IAM users in the management account are long-term credentials, which the company does not allow.",
      "Building and assigning a permission set during an incident is slow and error prone, and the responders may need other accounts as the investigation grows."] },

{ id: "d2-04", d: 2,
  q: "A company wants a forensics environment that is ready before any incident. Investigators must analyze copies of Amazon EBS volumes from compromised Amazon EC2 instances in any of its 40 accounts, without affecting production and without any internet access from the copies. The EBS volumes are encrypted with customer managed AWS KMS keys that are owned by each workload account.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select THREE.)",
  o: ["Create a dedicated forensics account with an isolated VPC that has no internet access and an AMI with forensic tools.",
      "Add the forensics account to the key policy of each customer managed key with permission to use the key for Amazon EBS.",
      "Share the EBS snapshots of the compromised volumes with the forensics account during an incident.",
      "Copy the snapshots with the AWS managed key for Amazon EBS, and share the copies with the forensics account.",
      "Attach forensic tools to the running production instances, and analyze the volumes in place in each account."],
  a: [0, 1, 2],
  r: ["A separate account keeps the evidence and the analysis away from production, and an isolated VPC stops malware on the copies from reaching the internet. A prepared AMI saves time during an incident.",
      "To create volumes from snapshots that are encrypted with a customer managed key, the forensics account needs permission in that key's policy, which can be granted in advance.",
      "Sharing the snapshots gives the forensics account copies of the volumes without touching the production instances.",
      "Snapshots encrypted with the AWS managed key cannot be shared with another account, so this does not work.",
      "Working on running production instances can change evidence and can alert the attacker."] },

{ id: "d2-05", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs a public application behind Amazon CloudFront and an Application Load Balancer and subscribes to AWS Shield Advanced. The company has a Business Support plan. The company wants the Shield Response Team (SRT) to contact its on-call engineers proactively during a large DDoS event that affects the application's availability.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Associate Amazon Route 53 health checks with the protected resources for health-based detection.",
      "Turn on proactive engagement, and add the on-call engineers as emergency contacts for the SRT.",
      "Grant the SRT access to the account with an IAM role, and allow access to the AWS WAF log bucket.",
      "Create a CloudWatch alarm on the DDoSDetected metric that opens an AWS Support case automatically.",
      "Turn on proactive engagement, and list only the account's root user email address as the contact."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Proactive engagement relies on health-based detection. Route 53 health checks that are associated with the protected resources tell Shield Advanced when an event affects the application.",
      "With proactive engagement turned on, the SRT contacts the listed emergency contacts directly when a health check shows that an event is affecting availability.",
      "SRT access lets the team help mitigate an event in the account. It does not make the SRT contact the company first.",
      "An automatic support case is the company contacting AWS, not the SRT contacting the on-call engineers.",
      "The root user email address does not reach the on-call engineers, so the SRT would contact the wrong people."] },

{ id: "d2-06", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs all of its workloads, including production, development, and a payment application that is in scope for PCI DSS, in a single AWS account. A recent incident in a development workload exposed IAM credentials that could read production data. The company wants to limit what credentials from one workload can reach in future incidents, and must keep central governance and billing.\n\nWhich strategy will MOST reduce the blast radius of a future incident?",
  o: ["Move the workloads into separate accounts in AWS Organizations by environment and data sensitivity. Apply SCPs to each organizational unit, and manage access through IAM Identity Center.",
      "Keep the single account. Add permissions boundaries to every IAM role, and use attribute-based access control with an Environment tag to separate the workloads.",
      "Keep the single account. Move each workload into its own VPC, and block network traffic between the VPCs with network ACLs and security groups.",
      "Move the payment application to a new standalone account outside the organization. Keep development and production together in the existing account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An AWS account is the strongest isolation boundary for IAM and resources. Separate accounts by environment and sensitivity, with SCPs on the organizational units, limit what leaked credentials from one workload can reach, while Organizations keeps governance and billing central.",
      "Boundaries and tags help, but every workload still shares one account, so one policy mistake or tag change can expose production again.",
      "VPCs separate network traffic, but IAM credentials call AWS APIs directly, so they still reach data in the same account.",
      "A standalone account loses central governance and billing, and development credentials could still reach production data."] },

{ id: "d2-07", d: 2,
  q: "A company has automated incident response runbooks that should start when Amazon EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group become unhealthy or when calls to an AWS API begin to fail. The company wants to test the detections and runbooks every month in a pre-production account in a controlled way. Any test must stop automatically if the application's error rate alarm goes into the ALARM state.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create AWS Fault Injection Service experiment templates for instance termination and API errors. Add the error rate alarm as a stop condition, and run them monthly.",
      "Create AWS Fault Injection Service experiment templates for instance termination and API errors. Run them monthly without stop conditions, and watch the error rate alarm.",
      "Add the application to AWS Resilience Hub, and run an assessment each month. Treat a passing assessment as proof that the detections and runbooks work.",
      "Write AWS Lambda functions that terminate instances and change IAM policies to cause API errors. Run the functions each month from an EventBridge schedule."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS Fault Injection Service runs controlled experiments from templates, such as terminating instances or injecting API errors. A CloudWatch alarm as a stop condition ends the experiment automatically when the error rate becomes too high.",
      "Without a stop condition, the experiment keeps running when the alarm fires, and a person must notice and stop it.",
      "An assessment checks the architecture against recovery targets. It does not inject faults, so it does not test the detections or runbooks.",
      "Custom functions that break resources have no automatic stop condition and are risky to maintain."] },

{ id: "d2-08", d: 2,
  q: "A company must confirm that a critical application can meet a recovery time objective (RTO) of 1 hour and a recovery point objective (RPO) of 15 minutes during an incident. The application runs on Amazon EC2, Amazon RDS, and Amazon S3 and is deployed with AWS CloudFormation. The company wants recommendations to close any gaps, and the check must run again after every deployment.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Add the application to AWS Resilience Hub from its CloudFormation stack with a resiliency policy for the RTO and RPO. Assess it from the pipeline after each deployment.",
      "Add the application to AWS Resilience Hub from its CloudFormation stack with a resiliency policy for the RTO and RPO. Run one assessment now, and run another each year.",
      "Create an AWS Backup plan that backs up every resource every 15 minutes. Treat successful backup jobs as proof that the application meets its RTO and RPO.",
      "Create AWS Fault Injection Service experiments that stop the EC2 instances after each deployment. Measure the recovery time from Amazon CloudWatch metrics."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Resilience Hub assesses an application against a resiliency policy that sets its RTO and RPO, reports whether the targets can be met, and recommends improvements. Running assessments from the pipeline repeats the check after every deployment.",
      "A yearly assessment does not check the application after every deployment.",
      "Successful backups say nothing about how long recovery takes, so they do not prove the RTO, and they give no recommendations.",
      "Stopping instances measures one recovery path. It does not check the RPO or give recommendations for the whole application."] },

{ id: "d2-09", d: 2,
  q: "A company has built an Amazon EventBridge rule that starts an incident response workflow for Amazon GuardDuty findings of the Backdoor:EC2/C&CActivity.B type. The security team must test the whole pipeline, from the finding through the rule to the workflow, in the production account. The test must not create real threats or run attack tools.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Generate GuardDuty sample findings for the Backdoor:EC2/C&CActivity.B type. Confirm that the rule matches them and that the workflow runs.",
      "Send a test event with the PutEvents API that has the source aws.guardduty and the finding's details. Confirm that the workflow runs.",
      "Use the EventBridge sandbox to test the rule's event pattern against a copy of the finding. Confirm that the pattern matches the event.",
      "Add the IP address of a test server to a GuardDuty threat list, and connect to it from a production instance. Confirm that the workflow runs."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["GuardDuty can generate sample findings of chosen types. They are sent to EventBridge like real findings and are marked as samples, so the team can test the rule and the workflow end to end without real threats.",
      "PutEvents rejects custom events whose source starts with aws., so the test event cannot look like a GuardDuty finding.",
      "The sandbox checks only whether a pattern matches a sample event. It does not run the rule's targets, so the workflow is not tested.",
      "A threat list match produces a different finding type, and connecting from production creates real network activity."] },

{ id: "d2-10", d: 2,
  q: "A company's security analysts want interactive incident response playbooks that combine written guidance with code that queries Amazon Security Lake data in Amazon Athena and calls AWS APIs. The playbooks must run inside AWS under IAM roles, must not use personal access keys, and must be easy for 15 analysts to update and share.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create Jupyter notebooks for the playbooks in Amazon SageMaker AI. Run them with an IAM execution role that has the permissions that the playbooks need.",
      "Create Jupyter notebooks for the playbooks, and run them on each analyst's laptop. Give each analyst an IAM user access key with the permissions that are needed.",
      "Create AWS Systems Manager Automation runbooks for the playbooks. Put the written guidance in the description of each runbook for the analysts to read.",
      "Create Amazon Athena saved queries for each playbook. Store the written guidance and the AWS CLI commands in the description of each saved query."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["SageMaker AI notebooks mix written guidance with runnable code that can query Athena and call AWS APIs. They run in AWS under an IAM execution role, and the notebooks are easy to share and update.",
      "Running notebooks on laptops with IAM user access keys uses the personal long-term credentials that the company wants to avoid.",
      "Automation runbooks run fixed steps. They are not interactive notebooks for exploring query results during an investigation.",
      "Saved queries cannot call AWS APIs, and commands in a description must be copied and run by hand."] },

{ id: "d2-11", d: 2,
  q: "A company must capture memory and disk evidence from an Amazon EC2 instance automatically when AWS Security Hub CSPM receives selected high-severity findings for that instance. The capture must happen before the instance is changed, and the evidence must be stored in a forensics account. The company prefers a solution that AWS provides over building its own orchestration.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Deploy the Automated Forensics Orchestrator for Amazon EC2 solution. Configure it to start on the selected Security Hub CSPM findings.",
      "Enable Amazon GuardDuty Malware Protection for EC2 with snapshot retention. Copy the retained scan snapshots to the forensics account as the evidence.",
      "Create an Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager policy that snapshots all instances every hour. Copy the latest snapshot to the forensics account when a finding arrives.",
      "Create an EventBridge rule for the findings that runs an Automation runbook to stop the instance and create an AMI. Share the AMI with the forensics account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Automated Forensics Orchestrator for Amazon EC2 is an AWS solution that starts from Security Hub findings. It captures memory and disk evidence from the instance, isolates it, and stores the evidence for investigation.",
      "Malware scan snapshots contain disk data only. They do not include memory, and they are taken for a malware scan, not on the selected findings.",
      "Hourly snapshots can be an hour old and contain no memory, so they miss the state at the time of the finding.",
      "Stopping the instance clears memory before it can be captured, and it changes the instance."] },

{ id: "d2-12", d: 2,
  q: "A company's incident response process has four steps: isolate a resource, collect evidence, wait for an approval from the security lead, and then run remediation. Each automated step must retry with backoff when it fails, the process must wait up to 24 hours for the approval, and the team wants a visual history of every run.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements with the LEAST custom code?",
  o: ["Build an AWS Step Functions Standard workflow with retries on each task and a callback task that waits for the security lead's task token.",
      "Build an AWS Step Functions Express workflow with retries on each task and a callback task that waits for the security lead's task token.",
      "Write one AWS Lambda function that runs the steps in order with retries and polls an Amazon DynamoDB item every minute for the approval.",
      "Chain Amazon EventBridge rules, with each step publishing an event that starts the next one. Have the security lead publish the approval event."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Standard workflows have built-in retries with backoff and a visual execution history, and they can run for up to a year. A callback task pauses until the approver returns the task token.",
      "Express workflows run for at most 5 minutes and do not support the callback pattern, so they cannot wait 24 hours for the approval.",
      "A Lambda function runs for at most 15 minutes, so it cannot wait 24 hours, and the retries and polling are custom code.",
      "Chained rules have no built-in retries across steps, no run history, and no state for the approval wait."] },

{ id: "d2-13", d: 2,
  q: "AWS notified a company through AWS Health that an IAM user's access key was found in a public code repository. The company wants every future notification of this type in its account to deactivate the exposed key within minutes and alert the security team. Other IAM users and other AWS Health events must not be affected.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create an Amazon EventBridge rule for the AWS Health event type AWS_RISK_CREDENTIALS_EXPOSED. Target automation that deactivates the named key and notifies the team.",
      "Create an Amazon EventBridge rule for all AWS Health events in the issue category. Target automation that deactivates the keys of every IAM user and notifies the team.",
      "Create an AWS Config rule that marks access keys older than 90 days as noncompliant. Add an automatic remediation that deactivates the keys and notifies the team.",
      "Create an Amazon EventBridge rule for GuardDuty findings about exposed credentials in code repositories. Target automation that deactivates the key and notifies the team."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS Health sends a risk event of this type when AWS finds exposed credentials, and the event reaches EventBridge. A rule for that event type can run automation that deactivates only the named key and notifies the team within minutes.",
      "Matching every issue event and deactivating every user's keys affects other users and other events, which the company does not allow.",
      "Key age does not show that a key was exposed, and a recently created key that leaked would stay active.",
      "GuardDuty does not scan public code repositories, so it does not produce findings about keys found there."] },

{ id: "d2-14", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs an application in us-east-1 and us-west-2 behind Amazon Route 53. During an incident in one Region, operators must be able to shift all traffic to the other Region within minutes. The failover must keep working even if the Route 53 control plane, or the control planes of other services in the impaired Region, are unavailable.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Use Amazon Application Recovery Controller routing controls with routing control health checks on the Route 53 records. Change the routing control states through the cluster endpoints.",
      "Use Route 53 failover records with health checks on each Region's endpoint. Edit the records in the Route 53 console to move traffic during an incident.",
      "Use Route 53 weighted records for the two Regions. Change the weights through the Route 53 API during an incident to send all traffic to one Region.",
      "Use AWS Global Accelerator with an endpoint group in each Region. Change the traffic dials through the Global Accelerator API during an incident."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Routing controls change the state of Route 53 health checks through a highly available cluster data plane. Failover then works without the Route 53 control plane or the control planes in the impaired Region.",
      "Editing records relies on the Route 53 control plane, which might be unavailable during the incident.",
      "Changing weights through the Route 53 API is also a control plane action.",
      "The Global Accelerator API is served from us-west-2, so it might not be available if us-west-2 is the impaired Region."] },

{ id: "d2-15", d: 2,
  q: "A company wants to prepare for incidents in which responders must investigate many of its 120 accounts quickly. Responders need read access to security-relevant data in every account, such as AWS CloudTrail settings, AWS Config history, and Amazon GuardDuty findings, but must not be able to change any resource. Accounts that are created later must get the same access automatically.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Deploy a read-only role with the SecurityAudit managed policy to all accounts by using a service-managed StackSet with automatic deployment.",
      "Deploy a role that uses the PowerUserAccess managed policy to all accounts with a service-managed CloudFormation StackSet that has automatic deployment turned on.",
      "Deploy a read-only role that uses the SecurityAudit managed policy to the current 120 accounts with a self-managed CloudFormation StackSet.",
      "Give each responder an IAM user in the management account that can assume the OrganizationAccountAccessRole role in every member account."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The SecurityAudit policy grants read access to security configuration and findings without permission to change resources. A service-managed StackSet with automatic deployment adds the role to every account, including new ones.",
      "PowerUserAccess allows changes to most resources, which the responders must not be able to make.",
      "A self-managed StackSet does not deploy to accounts that are created later without someone adding them.",
      "OrganizationAccountAccessRole has administrator permissions by default, and IAM users are long-term credentials."] },

{ id: "d2-16", d: 2,
  q: "A company's security analysts review AWS Security Hub CSPM findings in the console of a delegated administrator account. For selected findings, an analyst must be able to start a remediation workflow in AWS Step Functions with one action from the console. Findings that the analysts do not select must not start the workflow.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a Security Hub CSPM custom action. Create an Amazon EventBridge rule that matches the custom action's ARN and starts the Step Functions workflow.",
      "Create a Security Hub CSPM automation rule that matches the findings to remediate. Configure the automation rule to start the Step Functions workflow.",
      "Create an Amazon EventBridge rule that matches all imported Security Hub CSPM findings. Start the Step Functions workflow for each finding that matches.",
      "Create a Security Hub CSPM insight for the findings to remediate. Configure the insight to start the Step Functions workflow when an analyst opens it."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A custom action sends the findings that an analyst selects to EventBridge as an event that carries the custom action's ARN. A rule for that ARN starts the workflow only for selected findings.",
      "Automation rules update finding fields such as severity and workflow status. They cannot start workflows, and they act without an analyst's choice.",
      "Matching all imported findings starts the workflow for findings that no analyst selected.",
      "Insights group findings for viewing. They cannot start workflows."] },

{ id: "d2-17", d: 2,
  q: "A security engineer must collect evidence from a compromised Amazon EC2 Linux instance that is still running. The company wants to keep as much volatile evidence as possible and to stop the attacker from reaching other systems while the evidence is collected. The instance is managed by AWS Systems Manager.\n\nWhich sequence of actions should the security engineer follow?",
  o: ["Apply an isolation security group. Capture memory with a tool that runs through AWS Systems Manager, and then snapshot the EBS volumes.",
      "Snapshot the EBS volumes, and then stop the instance. Start the instance in an isolated subnet, and capture memory through Systems Manager.",
      "Apply an isolation security group. Reboot the instance to end the attacker's processes, and then capture memory and snapshot the EBS volumes.",
      "Create an AMI of the instance with the default reboot behavior. Launch a copy in an isolated subnet, and capture memory from the copy."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Isolation stops the attacker without destroying evidence. Memory is the most volatile evidence, so it is captured first while the instance runs, and the disks are snapshotted afterward.",
      "Stopping the instance clears memory, so the memory capture after the restart contains none of the attacker's activity.",
      "Rebooting clears memory and ends the processes that the investigation needs to examine.",
      "Creating an AMI reboots the instance by default, which clears memory, and a new copy has none of the original memory."] },

{ id: "d2-18", d: 2,
  q: "A security engineer finds that an Amazon EC2 instance is sending data to an unknown external IP address over an established TCP connection. The engineer replaces the instance's security groups with an isolation security group that has no inbound or outbound rules, but the existing connection continues. Other instances in the same subnet must keep working during the response.\n\nWhat should the security engineer do to stop the existing connection immediately?",
  o: ["Add inbound and outbound deny rules for the instance's private IP address to the subnet's network ACL, numbered before the allow rules.",
      "Add a second security group to the instance with an explicit deny rule for the external IP address, and keep the isolation security group attached.",
      "Add an outbound deny rule for all traffic to the subnet's network ACL, with a rule number lower than the existing allow rules, until the response ends.",
      "Wait for the security group change to apply to tracked connections, which takes up to 15 minutes for instances that are running."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Security groups are stateful, so a tracked connection can continue after the rules change. Network ACLs are stateless and check every packet, so deny rules for the instance's address stop the connection at once without affecting other instances.",
      "Security groups support only allow rules, so a deny rule cannot be added.",
      "A deny rule for all traffic in the subnet's network ACL also cuts off the other instances, which must keep working.",
      "Tracked connections are not cut by waiting. They continue while traffic flows, so the security group change alone does not stop them."] },

{ id: "d2-19", d: 2,
  q: "A company's security team finds that an IAM user's access keys were used by an attacker to launch Amazon EC2 instances in an unused Region. The team must stop all use of the user's credentials immediately, including any console session, while keeping the keys and the user's history available for the investigation.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Deactivate the IAM user's access keys instead of deleting them.",
      "Attach an inline policy to the IAM user that explicitly denies all actions.",
      "Delete the IAM user's access keys, and create new keys for the user.",
      "Attach an SCP to the account that denies all actions in the unused Region.",
      "Change the user's console password, and leave the access keys active."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Deactivating the keys stops their use immediately, and the keys stay available for the investigation, for example to match key IDs in AWS CloudTrail.",
      "An explicit deny overrides every allow, so the user can do nothing with any credential, including an open console session.",
      "Deleting the keys removes them from the investigation, and new keys give the user working credentials again.",
      "The attacker can keep using the keys in every other Region.",
      "The attacker used the access keys, which keep working when the password changes."] },

{ id: "d2-20", d: 2,
  q: "Amazon GuardDuty reports that temporary credentials for an IAM role that an application on Amazon ECS uses are being used from an IP address outside AWS. The stolen credentials are valid for up to 6 more hours. The security team must invalidate every session that was issued for the role before now, immediately, while the application keeps working by getting new credentials.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Revoke the role's active sessions, which denies requests from sessions with an aws:TokenIssueTime earlier than now.",
      "Change the role's maximum session duration to 1 hour, which shortens the remaining lifetime of the sessions that were already issued.",
      "Attach a policy to the role that denies all requests when the aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent condition key is false for the session.",
      "Attach a policy to the role that denies requests from the attacker's IP address with an aws:SourceIp condition for the session."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Revoking active sessions adds an inline policy that denies all actions for sessions whose aws:TokenIssueTime is before the revocation time. The stolen sessions stop working at once, and new sessions for the application still work.",
      "The maximum session duration applies to new sessions. Credentials that were already issued keep working until they expire.",
      "Role sessions from ECS tasks are not MFA authenticated, so this denies the application as well as the attacker.",
      "The attacker can call from another IP address, so the stolen sessions would still be valid."] },

{ id: "d2-21", d: 2,
  q: "An attacker modified a company's AWS Lambda function to send data to an external endpoint. The function is invoked by an Amazon SQS event source mapping and by Amazon API Gateway. The security team must stop all invocations immediately, from every trigger, while keeping the function's code, configuration, and versions unchanged for analysis.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Set the function's reserved concurrency to 0 to throttle every invocation from every trigger.",
      "Disable the SQS event source mapping, and keep the API Gateway integration in place for now.",
      "Publish a new version of the function with the original code, and point the function's alias to it.",
      "Remove the network and SQS permissions from the function's execution role until the investigation ends."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Reserved concurrency of 0 throttles every invocation, whatever the trigger, so the function stops running at once. The code, configuration, and versions stay as they are.",
      "API Gateway can still invoke the function, so not every invocation stops.",
      "Publishing a version and moving the alias changes the function, and triggers that call the modified version directly still run it.",
      "The execution role does not control network access, and changing it alters the configuration that the team must keep."] },

{ id: "d2-22", d: 2,
  q: "Amazon GuardDuty Runtime Monitoring reports that a pod in an Amazon EKS cluster is running a cryptocurrency miner. The pod belongs to a Deployment with three replicas on a managed node group. The security team must stop the miner's network traffic quickly, keep the pod running for memory analysis, and stop new workloads from being placed on the affected node.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Apply a Kubernetes network policy that denies all ingress and egress traffic for the pod.",
      "Cordon the worker node to stop Kubernetes from scheduling new pods on the node.",
      "Delete the pod, and let the Deployment create a replacement pod on another node.",
      "Drain the worker node, and let the Deployment move its pods to other nodes.",
      "Scale the Deployment to zero replicas, and keep the node in the node group."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["A deny-all network policy that selects the pod cuts its traffic, which stops the miner from reaching its pool, while the pod keeps running for analysis.",
      "Cordoning marks the node as unschedulable, so no new pods land on it, and the existing pod stays in place.",
      "Deleting the pod destroys the memory evidence, and the replacement runs the same image.",
      "Draining evicts the pod from the node, which ends it and loses the memory evidence.",
      "Scaling to zero removes all three pods, including the one under investigation."] },

{ id: "d2-23", d: 2,
  q: "A security engineer must assess the scope of an Amazon GuardDuty finding about unusual API calls by an IAM role in one of 40 accounts. The engineer needs the role's activity over the last 30 days, the IP addresses and user agents that it used, and related findings across the organization's accounts, without writing queries. Amazon Detective is enabled for the organization with a delegated administrator.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Open the finding in Amazon Detective from the administrator account. Review the role's profile and the finding group that contains the finding.",
      "Open the finding in Amazon Detective from the member account that owns the role. Review the role's profile for that one account.",
      "Open the role in IAM Access Analyzer. Review the unused access findings for the role over the last 30 days of activity.",
      "Open the role in AWS Config. Review the configuration timeline of the role and its policies over the last 30 days."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The Detective administrator account has the behavior graph for all member accounts. The role's profile shows its activity, IP addresses, and user agents over time, and finding groups link related findings across accounts.",
      "Member accounts do not have the organization's behavior graph, so they cannot see related findings in other accounts.",
      "Unused access findings show permissions that the role does not use, not its recent activity or related findings.",
      "AWS Config shows changes to the role's configuration, not the API calls it made or related findings."] },

{ id: "d2-24", d: 2,
  q: "During an incident, a security engineer must find every API call that a compromised access key made across 30 accounts in the past 60 days, including object-level calls to Amazon S3. The company has an AWS CloudTrail Lake organization event data store that collects management events and S3 data events. The answer is needed within an hour.\n\nWhat should the security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Run a SQL query in CloudTrail Lake on the organization event data store that filters on userIdentity.accessKeyId and the 60-day time range.",
      "Use CloudTrail Event history in each of the 30 accounts. Filter by the access key ID, and download the results from each of the accounts.",
      "Run a SQL query in CloudTrail Lake on the organization event data store that filters on userIdentity.principalId and the 60-day time range.",
      "Create a new organization trail with S3 data events, and search the delivered log files for the access key ID as they arrive."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The organization event data store already holds management and S3 data events from all accounts. One query on userIdentity.accessKeyId and the time range returns every call that the key made.",
      "Event history shows only management events, so the object-level S3 calls would be missing, and 30 separate downloads are slow.",
      "principalId identifies the user, so the query would also return calls that were made with the user's other keys.",
      "A new trail records only future events, not the past 60 days."] },

{ id: "d2-25", d: 2,
  q: "A company must store forensic artifacts, such as disk images and memory captures, for possible legal proceedings. The artifacts must not be changed or deleted by anyone, including administrators and the root user, for 3 years. Investigators must also be able to prove in court that each artifact is identical to what was collected.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Store the artifacts in an S3 bucket in a forensics account with Object Lock in compliance mode for 3 years.",
      "Calculate a SHA-256 hash of each artifact when it is collected, and record it in the chain of custody.",
      "Store the artifacts in an S3 bucket with Object Lock in governance mode and a 3-year retention period.",
      "Store the artifacts in an S3 bucket with S3 Versioning and MFA delete, and keep the MFA device in a safe.",
      "Encrypt the artifacts with a customer managed KMS key, and disable the key after each upload."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Compliance mode prevents anyone, including the root user, from deleting or overwriting the artifacts until the retention period ends, and a separate account limits who can reach them.",
      "A hash taken at collection and kept in the chain of custody lets investigators show later that an artifact has not changed.",
      "In governance mode, users with the s3:BypassGovernanceRetention permission can delete protected objects.",
      "The root user can still delete versions with the MFA device, so this does not stop everyone.",
      "Disabling the key makes the artifacts unreadable for investigators, and it does not prove integrity."] },

{ id: "d2-26", d: 2,
  q: "An attacker with stolen credentials overwrote about 40,000 objects in an Amazon S3 bucket with encrypted copies and left a ransom note. S3 Versioning was enabled on the bucket before the attack, and the attacker did not delete any object versions. The attacker's access has been revoked, and the company must restore the original data within one business day.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to recover the data?",
  o: ["Use S3 Batch Operations with a manifest of the affected keys to copy each object's previous version over its current version.",
      "Suspend S3 Versioning on the bucket. The previous version of each affected object then becomes the current version again.",
      "Add an S3 Lifecycle rule that expires current versions after 1 day. The previous versions then become the current versions.",
      "Use S3 Replication to copy the bucket's current versions to a new bucket, and point the applications to the new bucket."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The original data still exists as noncurrent versions. Copying the previous version of each object over the current version restores it, and Batch Operations runs that copy across 40,000 objects.",
      "Suspending versioning only stops new versions from being created. The encrypted versions stay current.",
      "In a versioned bucket, expiring a current version adds a delete marker on top of it. It does not make the previous version current again.",
      "Replication copies the current versions, which are the encrypted copies that the attacker wrote."] },

{ id: "d2-27", d: 2,
  q: "Malware infected an Amazon EC2 instance that runs a business application. AWS Backup creates a recovery point for the instance every night and keeps 35 days of recovery points. The investigation shows that the attacker first gained access 9 days ago through an unpatched web server vulnerability and installed persistence 7 days ago. Evidence has already been collected.\n\nWhich approach should the team use to recover the application?",
  o: ["Restore from a recovery point from before the initial access 9 days ago. Patch the web server vulnerability, and validate the instance before it returns to service.",
      "Restore from a recovery point that was created 8 days ago, before the persistence was installed. Patch the web server vulnerability, and return the instance to service.",
      "Restore from the most recent recovery point. Remove the persistence that the investigation found, and patch the web server vulnerability before the instance returns.",
      "Restore from a recovery point that was created before the initial access 9 days ago. Return it to service now, and patch the vulnerability in the next maintenance window."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A recovery point from before the initial access is the last known clean state. Patching the vulnerability that the attacker used and validating the instance before it returns prevents reinfection.",
      "The attacker already had access 8 days ago, so that recovery point may hold changes that the investigation has not found.",
      "Removing the known persistence does not remove changes that the investigation missed, so the instance cannot be trusted.",
      "The restored instance would still have the unpatched vulnerability, so the attacker could get back in before the maintenance window."] },

{ id: "d2-28", d: 2,
  q: "AWS Security Hub CSPM reports that an Amazon S3 bucket that holds customer records has been publicly readable through its bucket policy for 2 days. S3 server access logging was enabled on the bucket for the whole period. The security team must stop the exposure immediately and determine whether any objects were downloaded by unknown parties.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Turn on S3 Block Public Access for the bucket, and remove the public statement from the bucket policy.",
      "Query the server access logs for GET requests for the objects from requesters outside the company in the 2 days.",
      "Query the AWS CloudTrail management events for GetObject calls on the bucket from unknown IP addresses.",
      "Turn on Amazon Macie automated sensitive data discovery for the bucket to report which objects were downloaded.",
      "Turn on S3 Versioning for the bucket to record which objects each requester downloaded during the 2 days."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Block Public Access overrides public bucket policies, which stops the exposure at once. Removing the public statement fixes the underlying misconfiguration.",
      "Server access logs record each request with the requester and source IP address, so they show whether unknown parties downloaded objects.",
      "GetObject is an S3 data event, not a management event, so management events do not contain these calls.",
      "Macie reports which objects contain sensitive data. It does not report who downloaded them.",
      "Versioning records object changes, not reads, and turning it on now records nothing about the past 2 days."] },

{ id: "d2-29", d: 2,
  q: "AWS CloudTrail shows that the root user of a member account in AWS Organizations signed in from an unknown IP address and created an access key. The company controls the root user's email inbox, and centralized root access is not enabled for member accounts. The security team must remove the attacker's root access and find out what the attacker changed.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Reset the root user password through the email inbox, and delete the access key that the attacker created.",
      "Register MFA for the root user, and review CloudTrail for every action by the root user and the new access key.",
      "Deactivate the attacker's root access key, and keep the current root password until the review is finished.",
      "Create an IAM user with administrator access, and use it to change the root password and delete the key.",
      "Close the member account immediately to remove every resource that the attacker might control in it."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Resetting the password through the inbox and deleting the access key remove the two ways that the attacker can act as root.",
      "MFA blocks another sign-in with a stolen password, and the CloudTrail review shows what else the attacker changed, such as new users or roles.",
      "The attacker still knows the current password and can sign in again.",
      "IAM principals cannot change the root user's password or manage root access keys.",
      "Closing the account destroys evidence and production resources and does not show what the attacker did."] },

{ id: "d2-30", d: 2,
  q: "Amazon GuardDuty reports a CryptoCurrency:EC2/BitcoinTool.B finding for an Amazon EC2 instance in an Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer. The security team has captured the evidence it needs. The investigation found that the attacker exploited a package vulnerability in the group's 8-month-old AMI, and a newer, patched AMI is available.\n\nWhich action is the BEST way to recover the workload?",
  o: ["Update the launch template to the patched AMI, and keep the infected instance isolated outside the group. Replace the other instances with the patched AMI.",
      "Detach and isolate the infected instance, and let the group launch a replacement from the current launch template. Keep the other instances in service as they are.",
      "Stop the mining process with Systems Manager Run Command, and patch the package on the instance in place. Keep the instance in service in the group.",
      "Reboot the infected instance to stop the mining software. Update the launch template to the patched AMI for the instances that are launched in the future."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A compromised instance cannot be trusted after cleanup. Keeping it isolated outside the group preserves it, and replacing every instance from the patched AMI removes the vulnerability that the attacker used from the whole group.",
      "The replacement and the other instances still run the vulnerable AMI, so the attacker can return.",
      "Stopping one process and patching in place does not remove the attacker's access or any persistence.",
      "Malware on disk usually starts again after a reboot, and the infected instance stays in service."] },

{ id: "d2-31", d: 2,
  q: "Amazon GuardDuty reports that Amazon EC2 instances in 6 VPCs are querying a domain that is used for DNS data exfiltration. The instances use the Amazon Route 53 Resolver. The company must block resolution of this domain and all of its subdomains for the 6 VPCs immediately, without affecting any other DNS lookups, while the investigation continues.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a DNS Firewall rule group with a custom domain list that contains the domain and the wildcard *.domain, with a BLOCK action. Associate it with the 6 VPCs.",
      "Create a DNS Firewall rule group with a custom domain list that contains the domain and the wildcard *.domain, with an ALERT action. Associate it with the 6 VPCs.",
      "Add network ACL rules that deny outbound UDP and TCP port 53 in every subnet of the 6 VPCs. Keep the rules in place until the investigation ends.",
      "Create a Route 53 public hosted zone for the domain with no records. Associate the public hosted zone with the 6 VPCs until the investigation ends."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["DNS Firewall filters queries that go through the Route 53 Resolver. A custom domain list with the domain and its wildcard, and a BLOCK action, stops resolution of the domain and its subdomains in every associated VPC without affecting other lookups.",
      "The ALERT action only logs matching queries. It does not block them.",
      "Blocking port 53 breaks every DNS lookup, and queries to the Resolver are not filtered by network ACLs anyway.",
      "Public hosted zones cannot be associated with VPCs. Only private hosted zones can."] },

{ id: "d2-32", d: 2,
  q: "A company uses Amazon Security Lake for all 60 accounts in its organization, with VPC Flow Logs, AWS CloudTrail management events, and AWS WAF logs as sources. During an incident, a security engineer must find every record that involves a suspicious IP address across the three sources and all accounts in one result set. The engineer already has query access through a subscriber.\n\nWhat should the security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Run an Amazon Athena query that filters the OCSF src_endpoint.ip and dst_endpoint.ip fields in the Security Lake tables and combines the three sources.",
      "Run an Amazon Athena query on the Security Lake tables that filters each source's original field names, such as srcaddr for the flow logs, and combines the sources.",
      "Run a CloudWatch Logs Insights query in each account for the IP address. Combine the results from all of the accounts in a spreadsheet for the investigation.",
      "Search for the IP address in Amazon Detective. Use the Detective results as the list of every record from the three sources in all of the accounts."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Security Lake stores every source in the Open Cybersecurity Schema Framework (OCSF), so the endpoint IP fields have the same names in each table. One query can filter them and combine the sources across all accounts.",
      "Security Lake tables use OCSF field names, not each source's original names, so this query does not run as written.",
      "The data is in Security Lake, not in CloudWatch Logs in each account, and combining 60 result sets by hand is slow.",
      "Detective does not use AWS WAF logs as a source, and it shows entity activity rather than every record."] },

{ id: "d2-33", d: 2,
  q: "Amazon GuardDuty generates Recon:EC2/Portscan findings every night for one Amazon EC2 instance. A security engineer confirms that the instance runs the company's approved vulnerability scanner against the company's own subnets on a nightly schedule. Analysts review findings in the GuardDuty console and must stop seeing these expected findings, but the same finding type for any other instance must still appear.\n\nWhat should the security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Create a GuardDuty suppression rule that matches the Recon:EC2/Portscan finding type and the scanner instance's ID.",
      "Create a GuardDuty suppression rule that matches the Recon:EC2/Portscan finding type for every instance in the account.",
      "Add the scanner instance's private IP address to a GuardDuty trusted IP list for the account and the Region.",
      "Create an AWS Security Hub CSPM automation rule that sets the workflow status of these findings to SUPPRESSED."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A suppression rule archives new findings that match its criteria. Matching both the finding type and the scanner's instance ID hides only the expected findings, while the same type for other instances still appears.",
      "Without the instance ID, the rule also hides port scan findings for every other instance.",
      "Trusted IP lists accept only public IP addresses, and the scanner's private address cannot be added.",
      "Security Hub CSPM workflow status does not change what analysts see in the GuardDuty console."] },

{ id: "d2-34", d: 2,
  q: "During an investigation, a security engineer sees nine related Amazon GuardDuty findings over 3 days that involve the same IAM role, two Amazon EC2 instances, and one external IP address, across two accounts. The engineer must understand how the findings connect, which entities are involved, and the order of events. Amazon Detective is enabled for the organization.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Open the finding group in Amazon Detective from the administrator account. Review the group's findings, the involved entities, and the timeline.",
      "Create an AWS Security Hub CSPM insight that groups the findings by resource ID. Review the insight results for each of the entities in turn.",
      "Open each finding in the GuardDuty console of both accounts, and review its resource details. Sort the findings by their first seen time.",
      "Run a CloudTrail Lake query for the IAM role's activity over the 3 days. Join the results with the GuardDuty finding IDs in a spreadsheet."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Detective groups related findings across accounts by the entities that they share. A finding group shows the findings, the involved role, instances, and IP address, and a timeline of the activity.",
      "An insight counts findings by resource. It does not link the entities or show the order of events.",
      "Reviewing findings one by one in two consoles misses how the entities connect.",
      "A manual join of query results and finding IDs is slow and does not show the links that Detective builds."] },

{ id: "d2-35", d: 2,
  q: "A security group in a production VPC was changed at some point in the last week to allow inbound SSH from 0.0.0.0/0. A security engineer must determine which principal made the change, when it was made, and exactly which rules the security group had before the change. AWS CloudTrail and AWS Config are both enabled in the account.\n\nWhich solution will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Find the AuthorizeSecurityGroupIngress event in CloudTrail for the principal and the time. Review the security group's AWS Config timeline for the rules before the change.",
      "Find the AuthorizeSecurityGroupIngress event in CloudTrail for the principal, the time, and the full rule set that the security group had before the change.",
      "Query VPC Flow Logs for the first accepted SSH connection after the change. Use the flow log record to identify the principal and the earlier rules.",
      "Search the Amazon GuardDuty findings for the security group. Use the finding details for the principal, the time, and the security group's earlier rules."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudTrail records the AuthorizeSecurityGroupIngress call with the principal and the time. AWS Config records each configuration of the security group, so its timeline shows the complete rule set before and after the change.",
      "The CloudTrail event records only the rule that was added in the request, not the complete rule set from before.",
      "Flow logs record traffic metadata. They contain no principal and no security group rules.",
      "GuardDuty does not create findings for every security group change, and its findings do not hold earlier rule sets."] },

{ id: "d2-36", d: 2,
  q: "A company that subscribes to AWS Shield Advanced and has an Enterprise Support plan is under a large application layer DDoS attack on its Amazon CloudFront distribution. The protected application is slowing down, and the existing AWS WAF rate-based rules are not stopping the traffic. The company wants the fastest mitigation help from AWS.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Create an AWS Support case with critical severity to engage the Shield Response Team (SRT).",
      "Enable Shield Advanced automatic application layer DDoS mitigation for the distribution in Block mode.",
      "Enable Shield Advanced automatic application layer DDoS mitigation for the distribution in Count mode.",
      "Lower the existing rate-based rule's limit to the minimum value for every IP address that calls the application.",
      "Request a Shield Advanced cost protection credit now, and wait for the attack to end before changing rules."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Shield Advanced customers with Business or Enterprise Support can engage the SRT through a critical support case. The SRT analyzes the attack and helps create AWS WAF mitigations.",
      "In Block mode, automatic application layer mitigation creates, tests, and deploys AWS WAF rules that block the attack traffic on the protected resource.",
      "In Count mode, the automatic rules only count matching requests, so the attack traffic is not blocked.",
      "A very low limit for every IP address would also block legitimate users, and the attack may come from many addresses that each stay under the limit.",
      "Cost protection helps with charges afterward, but waiting does not mitigate the attack."] },

{ id: "d2-37", d: 2,
  q: "A developer pushed a database password to a public code repository 20 minutes ago. The password is stored in AWS Secrets Manager with rotation configured through a Lambda rotation function, and several applications read the secret at runtime. The security team must make the leaked password useless as quickly as possible without breaking the applications.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Start an immediate rotation of the secret in Secrets Manager. Review the database logs and AWS CloudTrail for any use of the leaked password.",
      "Change the secret's rotation schedule to every 4 hours. Wait for the next scheduled rotation to replace the leaked password in the database.",
      "Change the password directly in the database. Leave the secret value in Secrets Manager unchanged until the next scheduled rotation.",
      "Make the repository private. Keep the current password, because the repository is no longer visible to the public."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An immediate rotation runs the rotation function, which sets a new password in the database and in the secret, so the leaked value stops working while applications read the new one. The logs show whether anyone used it first.",
      "Waiting for a schedule leaves the leaked password valid for hours.",
      "The applications read the secret, which would still hold the old password, so they would fail to connect.",
      "Copies of the repository may already exist, so the leaked password must be treated as compromised."] },

{ id: "d2-38", d: 2,
  q: "AWS CloudTrail shows that an attacker with stolen administrator credentials called PutKeyPolicy on a customer managed AWS KMS key 2 hours ago. The new key policy allows an external AWS account to use the key for kms:Decrypt. The attacker's access has been revoked, and the key protects production data in Amazon S3 and Amazon EBS.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Restore the earlier key policy from the previous PutKeyPolicy event. Review CloudTrail for Decrypt calls from the external account in the 2 hours.",
      "Schedule the key for deletion with a 7-day waiting period. Create a new key, and encrypt the production data again with the new key.",
      "Turn on automatic key rotation for the key. The rotation ends the external account's ability to use the current key material.",
      "Disable the key until the investigation ends. Then enable the key again, and keep the key policy that the attacker set."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudTrail holds the key policy from earlier PutKeyPolicy or CreateKey events, so the correct policy can be restored, which removes the external account's access. The Decrypt events show whether the external account used the key.",
      "Deleting the key risks losing data that it protects, and re-encrypting everything is far more work than fixing the policy.",
      "Rotation changes the key material for new encryption, but the key policy still allows the external account to use the key.",
      "Disabling the key causes an outage, and enabling it again with the same policy restores the external account's access."] },

{ id: "d2-39", d: 2,
  q: "Amazon GuardDuty reports a suspicious network finding for an Amazon EC2 instance. The security team wants to confirm whether malware is present on the instance's Amazon EBS volumes before deciding how to respond. The team must not install an agent on the instance and must not stop the instance.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Start a GuardDuty Malware Protection for EC2 on-demand scan of the instance. Review the scan's malware findings.",
      "Start an Amazon Inspector agentless scan of the instance's volumes. Review the findings for malware files.",
      "Start a GuardDuty Malware Protection for S3 scan, and point it at a snapshot of the instance's volumes.",
      "Start an Amazon Macie sensitive data discovery job for the instance's volumes with the managed data identifiers."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Malware Protection for EC2 scans copies of the instance's EBS volumes without an agent and without stopping the instance, and it reports any malware that it finds.",
      "Amazon Inspector agentless scanning finds software vulnerabilities, not malware files.",
      "Malware Protection for S3 scans objects that are uploaded to S3 buckets, not EBS snapshots.",
      "Macie analyzes objects in S3 for sensitive data. It does not scan EBS volumes or look for malware."] },

{ id: "d2-40", d: 2,
  q: "After containing an incident in an AWS account, a security engineer must find and remove any persistence that the attacker created during the 5-day incident window, such as new IAM users, access keys, roles, trust policy changes, and AWS Lambda functions. The attacker used two compromised IAM users and one role. An organization trail logs all management events.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Search CloudTrail for create, update, and attach events by the three principals in the window. Remove or revert everything that they created or changed.",
      "Search CloudTrail for create events by the three compromised principals in the window. Remove each resource that they created, and leave the resources that they updated unchanged.",
      "Review the IAM credential report and the IAM Access Analyzer unused access findings. Remove the users and roles that have not been used during the last 5 days.",
      "Delete every IAM user, role, and Lambda function that was created in the account in the last 30 days. Ask the application teams to re-create the ones that they need."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["CloudTrail records each call with the principal that made it. Filtering on the attacker's principals and the window shows what they created and what they changed, such as trust policies, so every piece of persistence can be removed or reverted.",
      "Persistence can hide in changes to existing resources, such as a role trust policy that now trusts an outside account, so updates must be reverted too.",
      "Unused access findings show unused permissions, not what an attacker created, and attacker resources may have been used recently.",
      "Mass deletion breaks legitimate workloads and still misses changes to older resources."] },

{ id: "d2-41", d: 2,
  q: "A company's production workloads are defined in AWS CloudFormation templates in a version-controlled repository, and all changes normally go through a pipeline. An attacker changed several resources directly in the console, including security groups and an IAM role trust policy. The incident is contained, and evidence has been collected.\n\nWhich approach will restore the environment to a trusted state MOST reliably?",
  o: ["Redeploy the workloads into new stacks from the known good templates through the pipeline, confirm them with drift detection, and retire the changed stacks.",
      "Run drift detection on the stacks to list the changed resources. Copy the drifted settings into the templates to match production, and keep the changed stacks running.",
      "Run drift detection on the stacks, and accept the current state as the new baseline. Add AWS Config rules that detect any further changes to the resources.",
      "Ask the team to undo each change in the console from memory. Compare the result with a screenshot of the earlier setup, and keep the changed stacks running."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Templates in version control describe the trusted configuration. New stacks built from them through the pipeline contain none of the attacker's changes, and drift detection confirms that they match the templates before the changed stacks are retired.",
      "Copying the drifted settings into the templates makes the attacker's changes part of the trusted configuration.",
      "Accepting the drift leaves the attacker's changes, such as the trust policy, in production.",
      "Manual repair from memory can miss changes, and a screenshot is not a reliable record of every setting."] },

{ id: "d2-42", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs automated remediation from an AWS Lambda function in a central security account. The function's execution role allows ec2:ModifyInstanceAttribute and ec2:CreateSnapshot on all resources. The function fails with UnauthorizedOperation when it tries to isolate Amazon EC2 instances in member accounts of the company's organization.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to resolve this issue?",
  o: ["Deploy a role in each member account that trusts the function's execution role. Have the function assume that role before it acts.",
      "Add a resource-based policy to the EC2 instances in each member account that allows the function's execution role to modify the instances.",
      "Add the member account IDs to the Resource element of the execution role's policy in the central account, and keep the same actions.",
      "Share the member account instances with the security account by using AWS RAM, and keep the function's code unchanged."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["An identity policy in one account cannot grant access to resources in another account that has no resource policy. The function must assume a role in each member account that trusts its execution role and has the EC2 permissions.",
      "EC2 instances do not support resource-based policies.",
      "The execution role's policy already covers all resources, and a policy in the central account cannot grant access inside other accounts.",
      "AWS RAM does not share EC2 instances."] },

{ id: "d2-43", d: 2,
  q: "An Amazon EC2 instance in a production VPC has an Amazon Inspector finding for a critical, network-exploitable vulnerability in its web server. Amazon GuardDuty now reports outbound traffic from the same instance to a known command and control IP address. The company uses AWS Systems Manager for all instances, and no evidence has been collected yet.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do FIRST?",
  o: ["Isolate the instance with a security group that allows only the forensic tools, and collect evidence before patching.",
      "Patch the web server with Systems Manager Patch Manager immediately, and close the GuardDuty finding after the patch succeeds.",
      "Terminate the instance immediately, and let the Auto Scaling group launch a replacement from the same AMI for the users.",
      "Add the command and control IP address to a GuardDuty threat list, and keep the instance in service while the team watches."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Together the two findings suggest that the vulnerability has already been exploited. Isolating the instance first stops further harm while preserving evidence, and patching comes after collection.",
      "Patching does not remove an attacker who is already on the instance, and it changes the evidence.",
      "Terminating destroys the evidence, and a replacement from the same AMI has the same vulnerability.",
      "A threat list only adds findings. The instance keeps talking to the attacker while it stays in service."] },

{ id: "d2-44", d: 2,
  q: "Amazon GuardDuty reports that credentials from an Amazon EC2 instance role were used from an IP address outside AWS. The security engineer confirms that the application on the instance has a server-side request forgery (SSRF) flaw and that the instance still allows IMDSv1. A fix for the application will take two weeks.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Revoke active sessions for the instance role to invalidate the stolen credentials.",
      "Modify the instance metadata options to require IMDSv2 session tokens.",
      "Set the instance metadata hop limit to 64, and keep IMDSv1 enabled.",
      "Detach and reattach the instance profile to rotate the role's credentials.",
      "Turn off the instance metadata service on the instance permanently."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Revoking active sessions denies every role session issued before the revocation, so the stolen credentials stop working.",
      "IMDSv2 requires a session token from a PUT request, which typical SSRF flaws cannot obtain, so the same theft is much harder during the two weeks.",
      "A higher hop limit makes the metadata service easier to reach and does nothing about IMDSv1.",
      "Reattaching the profile does not invalidate credentials that the attacker already holds.",
      "The application needs the role credentials, so turning off the metadata service breaks it."] },

{ id: "d2-45", d: 2,
  q: "During an investigation, a security engineer must find all log events that contain one request ID across 14 AWS Lambda functions and an Amazon ECS service. The logs are in 15 separate Amazon CloudWatch Logs log groups in one account and Region, and the events are from the last 6 hours. The engineer must have the results within minutes.\n\nWhat should the security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Run one CloudWatch Logs Insights query across all 15 log groups that filters @message for the request ID.",
      "Create a metric filter for the request ID in each of the 15 log groups, and review the resulting metrics for the last 6 hours.",
      "Create a subscription filter on each log group that sends events with the request ID to a Lambda function that stores them.",
      "Create an export task to Amazon S3 for each log group, and search the exported files with Amazon Athena when the exports finish."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A Logs Insights query can run across many log groups at once. Filtering @message for the request ID returns the matching events from every function and the service together, in seconds.",
      "Metric filters count events that arrive after they are created, so they cannot find events from the last 6 hours.",
      "Subscription filters send only new events, so the past 6 hours of events would be missed.",
      "Export tasks run one at a time in an account, so 15 exports and a query take far longer than minutes."] },

{ id: "d2-46", d: 2,
  q: "Amazon Macie reports a SensitiveData:S3Object/Financial finding for 300 objects in an Amazon S3 bucket. A security engineer must decide quickly whether this is a real exposure of credit card data or a false positive, such as test card numbers, before the team chooses a response.\n\nWhat should the security engineer review FIRST?",
  o: ["The finding's details, including where the sensitive data occurs and the bucket's public access and sharing settings.",
      "The Macie policy findings for the bucket, including any finding about the bucket's encryption settings and its Object Lock retention settings.",
      "The S3 Storage Lens metrics for the bucket, including the object count and the storage class of each of the 300 affected objects.",
      "The AWS Config timeline for the bucket, including all changes to the bucket's lifecycle rules over the last 90 days of the bucket."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A sensitive data finding includes where the data occurs in each object, the type and count of the data, and the bucket's public access and sharing settings. These details show whether the numbers are real and whether they are exposed.",
      "Policy findings describe bucket settings, not the data that triggered this finding.",
      "Storage metrics say nothing about whether the data is real or exposed.",
      "Lifecycle changes do not show whether the objects hold real card numbers or who can reach them."] },

{ id: "d2-47", d: 2,
  q: "Malware has spread to Amazon EC2 instances in 12 VPCs that send all internet traffic through a central inspection VPC with AWS Network Firewall. The malware contacts a known command and control domain over HTTPS, and the IP addresses behind the domain change every few minutes. All other HTTPS traffic must keep working.\n\nWhich action will contain the outbound traffic for all of the VPCs MOST quickly?",
  o: ["Add a stateful domain list rule group that denies the command and control domain by matching the TLS SNI.",
      "Add a stateless rule to the firewall policy that drops traffic to the IP addresses that the domain resolves to at this moment.",
      "Add a stateful domain list rule group to the firewall policy that denies the domain by matching only the HTTP Host header.",
      "Change the security groups of every instance in the 12 VPCs to deny outbound traffic to the command and control domain."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The central firewall already sees the outbound traffic of every VPC. A domain list rule that matches the TLS Server Name Indication (SNI) blocks HTTPS traffic to the domain wherever its IP addresses move, with one change.",
      "The IP addresses change every few minutes, so a rule for the current addresses soon stops matching.",
      "HTTPS traffic carries the host name in the TLS SNI, not in a readable HTTP Host header, so this rule would not match it.",
      "Security groups have no deny rules and cannot match domain names, and changing every instance is slow."] },

{ id: "d2-48", d: 2,
  q: "A security engineer must preserve the AWS CloudTrail log files for a 3-day incident window as evidence. The trail has log file integrity validation turned on. Legal counsel requires proof that the log files were not changed after AWS delivered them, and the evidence copies must not be deletable for 7 years.\n\nWhat should a security engineer do to meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Copy the log files and their digest files to an evidence bucket with Object Lock in compliance mode. Run the AWS CLI validate-logs command.",
      "Copy only the log files to an evidence bucket with S3 Object Lock in compliance mode. Calculate new SHA-256 hashes of the copies as the proof of integrity.",
      "Copy the log files and the matching digest files to an evidence bucket with S3 Versioning turned on. Run the AWS CLI validate-logs command for the window.",
      "Turn off the trail to stop any further writes to its bucket. Keep the original log files in the trail's bucket for legal counsel to review."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The digest files are signed by CloudTrail, and validate-logs uses them to prove that each log file is unchanged since delivery. Compliance mode keeps the copies from being deleted for the retention period.",
      "Hashes calculated now prove only that the copies match each other. They do not prove that the files are unchanged since AWS delivered them.",
      "Versioning keeps old versions, but authorized users can still delete them, so it does not meet the 7-year requirement.",
      "Turning off the trail stops new logging and does not prove or protect the existing files."] },

{ id: "d2-49", d: 2,
  q: "After an incident in which an attacker used an overly permissive IAM role in a workload account, the company holds a lessons-learned review. The investigation found that the role had permissions for 40 services but used only 3, and that the attacker's first actions went undetected for 2 days. The company wants to prevent the same kind of incident and to detect it sooner.\n\nWhich combination of steps will meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Reduce the role's permissions to least privilege based on IAM Access Analyzer unused access findings.",
      "Update the incident response runbooks with the gaps that were found, and add detections for the attacker's techniques.",
      "Add a permissions boundary to the role that allows all 40 services, and keep the role's policies unchanged.",
      "Turn off the IAM Access Analyzer unused access analyzer after the review, and turn it on again after the next incident.",
      "Move the role to a new account, and keep its permissions for all 40 services for the same workload."],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Unused access findings show which permissions the role never used, so the team can remove them and shrink what an attacker could do with the role.",
      "Lessons learned should change the runbooks and add detections, so the same technique is caught earlier next time.",
      "A boundary that allows all 40 services removes nothing from what an attacker could do.",
      "Turning the analyzer off removes the visibility that keeps permissions tight between incidents.",
      "A new account with the same permissions keeps the same risk."] },

{ id: "d2-50", d: 2,
  q: "A security engineer must contain an Amazon EC2 instance that might be compromised. The instance is one of four in an Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer, and the service must keep its full capacity while the investigation continues. The group replaces instances that fail Elastic Load Balancing health checks.\n\nWhich approach will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["Detach the instance from the Auto Scaling group without decrementing the desired capacity. Apply an isolation security group, and let the group launch a replacement.",
      "Detach the instance from the Auto Scaling group and decrement the desired capacity. Apply an isolation security group, and keep the other three instances in service.",
      "Apply an isolation security group, and keep the instance in the Auto Scaling group. Let the load balancer stop sending traffic to the instance.",
      "Turn on scale-in protection for the instance. Apply an isolation security group, and keep the instance registered with the load balancer."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Detaching without decrementing the desired capacity removes the instance from the group and the load balancer, and the group launches a replacement to keep full capacity. The isolated instance is preserved for the investigation.",
      "Decrementing the desired capacity leaves the service with three instances instead of four.",
      "The isolated instance fails its health checks, so the group terminates and replaces it, which destroys the evidence.",
      "Scale-in protection does not stop health check replacement, so the group can still terminate the isolated instance."] },
]);
